{
	title: 'OpenAI native search when Serper is unavailable',
	spec: 'OpenAI requests with Hal tools offer provider-hosted web_search alongside the tool metatool only for an explicit supported-model allowlist and only when Serper has no usable key or GOOGLE is absent from the active tool registry. Configured, enabled Serper remains preferred; request failures do not trigger fallback. Requests without Hal tools and other providers remain unchanged. The allowlist is shared by API and subscription transports and is extensible through the provider hook. Each native search is a hosted_tool block shown with the existing tool card: the header names the query, the body lists extra queries and the sources. Hal never executes it, and replay drops it. Citations stay in the answer text. A failed search is an error card with the complete provider event. This replaces the g6 prohibition of provider search and makes hosted search an exception to the single native tool rule of 3fv. Verify subscription search against the live endpoint before enabling it; preserve focused offline regression coverage.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Verified live on 14 Oct: GPT-6.1 Sol performed native search on the subscription HTTP endpoint and API endpoint; GPT-5.3 Codex performed search on the API endpoint but the subscription endpoint rejected that model as unavailable. The normal subscription WebSocket request with both tool and web_search also searched successfully, and its follow-up replay reused the answer without another search. Other allowlisted current GPT models rely on documented support rather than individual probes. Search activity uses existing assistant text instead of new client protocol types. The focused OpenAI suite has 15 passing tests; targeted provider lint passes. Full typecheck reports existing errors in unrelated plugins and tools/bash.ts.',
		'A search-activity line adds paragraph breaks only next to text in the same block, so search-only replies have no leading blank lines.',
		'Provider-run tools are hosted_tool assistant blocks (src/common/blocks.ts): the transcript shows each as a finished tool card (call plus result, existing card style), Hal never executes it, and replay drops it so no provider receives a call it did not issue. Message blocks are ReplayBlock, so providers cannot receive one. OpenAI web_search cards show the query in the header; the body shows extra queries and the sources, requested with include web_search_call.action.sources (accepted by the subscription endpoint, checked live). A failed search is an error card with the raw provider event; the turn continues. This replaces search lines in assistant text.'
	],
